.Dd September 16, 2026
.Dt KACHE 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kache
.Nd multi threaded, file backed key/value cache with an HTTP interface
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl mPHnFqvh
.Op Fl l Ar addr
.Op Fl p Ar port
.Op Fl f Ar file
.Op Fl s Ar size
.Op Fl S Ar shards
.Op Fl t Ar threads
.Op Fl c Ar conns
.Op Fl e Ar ttl
.Op Fl y Ar ms
.Op Fl i Ar idle
.Op Fl b Ar backlog
.Op Fl B Ar bytes
.Op Fl K Ar bytes
.Op Fl V Ar bytes
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
keeps a key/value cache in a file mapped
.Dv MAP_SHARED ,
so the data lives in the page cache while it is hot and survives a
restart without a serialisation step.  Entries carry an optional time to
live and a version used for compare and swap.  Clients speak HTTP/1.1.
.Pp
The store is divided into independently locked shards, each with its own
hash index and allocation arena.  When a shard runs out of room it evicts
approximately least recently used entries, preferring ones that have
already expired.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl l Ar addr
Address to listen on.  Default
.Ql 0.0.0.0 .
.It Fl p Ar port
Port to listen on.  Default
.Ql 7070 .
.It Fl f Ar file
Backing file.  Created if absent.  Default
.Ql kache.db .
.It Fl s Ar size
Size of the backing file, with an optional
.Ql k , m , g
or
.Ql t
suffix.  This is roughly how much the cache holds.  Default
.Ql 1G .
.It Fl S Ar shards
Number of shards, rounded down to a power of two.  Zero derives one from
the file size.  Default zero.
.It Fl t Ar threads
Worker threads.  Zero means one per CPU.  Default zero.
.It Fl c Ar conns
Maximum concurrent connections per thread.  Default
.Ql 8192 .
.It Fl e Ar ttl
Time to live in seconds applied to requests that do not carry one.  Zero
means entries do not expire by default.  Default zero.
.It Fl y Ar ms
How often to
.Xr msync 2
the mapping in the background.  Zero leaves writeback entirely to the
kernel.  Default
.Ql 1000 .
.It Fl i Ar idle
Seconds a connection may sit idle before it is closed.  Default
.Ql 60 .
.It Fl b Ar backlog
Listen backlog.  Default
.Ql 1024 .
.It Fl B Ar bytes
Expected size of a stored item, header and key included.  Enough buckets
are provisioned that an arena full of items this size can still be
indexed at the table's load factor, so it decides the split between index
and data and nothing else.  Default
.Ql 128 .
.It Fl K Ar bytes
Largest key accepted.  Default
.Ql 255 .
.It Fl V Ar bytes
Largest value accepted.  Also bounds how many shards fit, since a shard's
arena must hold several maximum sized items.  Default
.Ql 256k .
.It Fl m
Lock the mapping into memory with
.Xr mlock 2
so it can never be paged out.  Needs a sufficient
.Dv RLIMIT_MEMLOCK .
.It Fl P
Fault the whole file in at startup instead of on demand.
.It Fl H
Advise the kernel that the mapping may use transparent huge pages.
.It Fl n
Discard any existing contents and create the store afresh.  Required to
change the geometry of an existing file.
.It Fl F
Enable
.Ql POST /flush .
Without it that endpoint answers 403.
.It Fl q
Quiet; suppress informational messages.
.It Fl v
Print the version and exit.
.It Fl h
Print a usage summary and exit.
.El
.Sh HTTP INTERFACE
.Bl -tag -width "POST /prepend/<key>" -compact
.It Cm GET Ar /kv/<key>
Fetch.  The
.Ql ETag
is the compare and swap token.
.It Cm HEAD Ar /kv/<key>
Metadata only.
.It Cm PUT Ar /kv/<key>
Store the request body.
.It Cm POST Ar /kv/<key>
As
.Cm PUT .
.It Cm DELETE Ar /kv/<key>
Remove.
.It Cm POST Ar /incr/<key>
Add
.Ql ?by=N
to a numeric value.
.It Cm POST Ar /decr/<key>
Subtract
.Ql ?by=N .
.It Cm POST Ar /append/<key>
Append the body.
.It Cm POST Ar /prepend/<key>
Prepend the body.
.It Cm POST Ar /touch/<key>
Reset the time to live.
.It Cm POST Ar /mget
One percent encoded key per line; the reply frames each value as its
length, a newline and that many bytes, or
.Ql -1
for a miss.
.It Cm POST Ar /mset
Records of
.Ql <key> <bytes> [ttl]
followed by that many bytes.
.It Cm POST Ar /mdel
One key per line.
.It Cm POST Ar /flush
Drop everything.
.It Cm GET Ar /stats
Counters, one per line.
.It Cm GET Ar /metrics
The same in Prometheus form.
.It Cm GET Ar /health
Liveness.
.El
.Pp
.Ql ?ttl=<seconds>
or
.Ql ?ttlms=<milliseconds>
sets the expiry, zero meaning never.
.Ql If-None-Match: *
stores only when the key is absent and
.Ql If-Match: \(dq<etag>\(dq
only when the value has not changed since it was read; both answer 412
when the condition fails.  Every operation is atomic with respect to
every other operation on the same key.
.Pp
The batch endpoints exist because a round trip costs far more than a
lookup does.  They are not atomic across keys: each key is taken under
its own shard lock in turn.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa config.def.h
Compile time defaults and limits.  Copied to
.Pa config.h
by the build, which is the file to edit.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Serve a four gigabyte cache with a default expiry of five minutes:
.Pp
.Dl $ kache -f /var/tmp/kache.db -s 4G -e 300
.Pp
Store and read a value:
.Pp
.Dl $ curl -X PUT -d hello localhost:7070/kv/greeting
.Dl $ curl localhost:7070/kv/greeting
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
On startup after an unclean shutdown
.Nm
reports that it is rebuilding the index, and how many records survived.
A shard whose block chain fails validation is reset and reported
separately.
.Sh BUGS
Only one process may have a store file open at a time, which is enforced
with an advisory lock.  Durability is best effort: writes reach disk when
the kernel or
.Fl y
says so, and a crash can lose the most recent ones.
